# ML Project Lifecycle — Standing Conventions

Every ML project in this repo follows the same 12-step lifecycle. This is the
authoritative framework — treat CRISP-DM/TDSP as background theory only, not
the literal phase list to implement. Do not invent alternate phase names or
skip steps; if a step is genuinely not applicable, say so explicitly in
`PROJECT.md` rather than silently omitting it.

## The 12 steps

1. Problem Formulation
2. Data Gathering & Structuring
3. Data Exploration
4. Feature Engineering
5. Multi-Model Training
6. Model Evaluation
7. Model Interpretability
8. Model Calibration
9. Model Documentation
10. Model Integration
11. Model Monitoring
12. Periodic Retraining (loops back to step 2/3)

## Phase-gating

Do not advance to the next step until the current step's definition-of-done
(below, where one is specified) is satisfied **and** that step's tests
pass (`python -m pytest` — later-step classes skip until their artifacts
exist). When asked to "move on" before that, flag the gap rather than
silently proceeding.

## Tests at each step

Tests are part of the stack, not optional polish. `/ml-init` copies
`templates/tests/` into the project `tests/` folder. After each skill,
update those tests if paths/names changed, then run pytest. Do not wrap
the skill until the matching class in `test_lifecycle_steps.py` passes:

| After | Class | Contract |
|---|---|---|
| `/ml-init` | `TestStep00Init` | scaffold dirs + `PROJECT.md` |
| `/ml-define` | `TestStep01Define` | target, success criteria, business constraint |
| `/ml-data` | `TestStep02Data` | OOT windows, seed, disjoint ids |
| `/ml-prep` | `TestStep04Prep` | aligned feature tables, no unseen train cats |
| `/ml-model` | `TestStep05Model` | RF + XGBoost + CatBoost, eval metrics logged |
| `/ml-evaluate` | `TestStep06Evaluate` | 21-row FPR operating table, champion |
| `/ml-explain` | `TestStep07Explain` | TP/FP/FN sort order, TreeSHAP importance |
| `/ml-calibrate` | `TestStep08Calibrate` | 1–1000 bands, 8.5 summary |
| `/ml-document` | `TestStep09Document` | report has no `Fill in:` prompts |
| `/ml-integrate` | `TestStep10Integrate` | scoring contract fields, threshold set |
| `/ml-monitor` | `TestStep11Monitor` | PSI alerts, `retrain_candidate` |
| `/ml-retrain` | `TestStep12Retrain` | promotion gate vs old model on new test |

## Stack defaults

- Language: Python + SQL. No other primary languages unless the user asks.
- Step 5 (Multi-Model Training) default bake-off: **Random Forest + XGBoost +
  CatBoost**, minimum three models compared. AutoGluon is *not* part of the
  default trio — reach for it only for a quick-and-dirty first baseline or a
  more advanced automated-ensembling pass, and only when asked or when it's
  clearly the fastest path to an early signal.
- Explainability: TreeSHAP by default for both global and local explanations
  (CatBoost/XGBoost have native SHAP support; for RF or AutoGluon models, use
  the `shap` TreeExplainer path). Do not substitute LIME or other
  explainability libraries unless asked.
- Experiment tracking: start with structured local run logs (JSON/YAML per
  run under `experiments/`, one file per run capturing params, metrics, data
  version, and git commit). Treat MLflow as the upgrade path once the project
  has enough runs to need querying/comparison UI — don't reach for MLflow on
  day one unless the user asks for it up front.

## Cloud decision

The cloud decision is made **once, up front** (at `/ml-init` or `/ml-define`
time) — never inferred mid-project from data size or runtime heuristics.
Confirm: is this project running in the cloud at all, and if so, which
provider (GCP / AWS / Azure / other)? If yes, default compute-heavy steps
(large-scale data prep, step 5 training, hyperparameter search) to a
cloud-first execution pattern for that provider rather than local execution,
and scaffold the matching cloud config directory. If no cloud was chosen,
everything stays local-first in Python and cloud should not be introduced
without re-confirming with the user.

## Sampling strategy

Default splitting method is **Out-of-Time (OOT)**, not random shuffling —
temporal order matters and the split must respect it. Record, as part of
Step 2 (Data Gathering & Structuring):

- **Seed:** the random seed used for any stochastic step (model training,
  any within-window subsampling).
- **Train window:** the date range used to fit candidate models.
- **Eval window:** the date range used for hyperparameter tuning / model
  selection — comes after the train window, never overlapping it.
- **Test window:** the most recent date range, held out and untouched until
  final evaluation (steps 6-8) — the forward-looking OOT holdout that keeps
  the eventual performance numbers honest.

Do not perform Step 3 (Data Exploration) on the eval or test windows beyond
confirming they exist and look structurally sane — exploring them defeats
the point of holding them out. Every downstream artifact (step 5 training,
step 6 evaluation, step 7 interpretability, step 8 calibration) should state
plainly which window(s) it was computed on.

## Step 6 definition-of-done — Model Evaluation

- Performance table: ROC-AUC, Precision, Recall, F1 (with a one-line plain-
  English explanation of F1 alongside it).
- ROC chart annotated at a chosen score threshold, stating where that
  threshold lands on FPR/TPR for the test set.
- Precision-Recall chart annotated at the same threshold, translated into
  precision/recall values.
- **Operating table** (this repo's term for the threshold/operating-point
  analysis): swept by **FPR from 0% to 5% in 0.25% increments**. For each FPR
  step, back out the corresponding score threshold, TPR, precision, recall,
  and F1. This is the standard operating-point artifact — do not substitute
  a generic "threshold vs. accuracy" table for it.
- Document how hyperparameters were chosen, and whether enough tuning trials
  were run to trust the result (not just "we ran a grid search").

## Step 7 definition-of-done — Model Interpretability

- Global: variable importance, plus PDP/ALE/ICE on the top variables, driven
  off TreeSHAP.
- Local: three specific cohorts, each top-N, sorted exactly as follows:
  - **TP** — actual positive class, ordered by the positive-class predicted
    score (`pred_1`) **descending**.
  - **FP** — actual negative class but high-scoring, ordered by `pred_1`
    **descending**.
  - **FN** — actual positive class but low-scoring, ordered by `pred_1`
    **ascending**.
- Bias probe: examine the TP/FP/FN cohorts above for shared attributes —
  this is a fairness check, not optional polish.

## Step 8 definition-of-done — Model Calibration

Two distinct deliverables — do not merge this into step 6's operating table:

- A calibration curve / reliability diagram for the model's raw scores.
- Score normalization: a rank-based spline mapping the raw model score onto
  a normalized 1-1000 scale, banded by population percentile. Starting
  bands (adjust per project, these are a default not a law):
  - 1000-900: top 0.25% of records (rarest / highest-risk)
  - 900-800: next 0.25%
  - 800-500: next 4.5%
  - 500-0: remaining ~95%

## Step 8.5 — Final Fit for Deployment

Once the champion model, its hyperparameters, and its calibration are
locked in from steps 5-8, refit that exact model (same architecture, same
hyperparameters — do not re-tune here) on the **full dataset**: train + eval
+ test windows combined. This is the model that actually gets deployed —
there's no reason to withhold the eval/test data from the production model
once it's no longer needed for honest evaluation. Document this as an
explicit checkpoint between Step 8 and Step 9 in every project, not an
implicit detail buried in code.

**Does calibration change?** Not necessarily, but don't assume — check it:

1. Re-score the test-window records using the newly refit model.
2. Compare that score distribution to the original test-window scores from
   the pre-refit (train-only) model.
3. If the distributions are close, carry the existing calibration mapping
   and operating table forward as-is.
4. If they've shifted meaningfully, recalibrate using the test-window
   scores from the refit model against actual outcomes — flag that this is
   slightly optimistic since those records were also used to fit the model
   being calibrated. For a stricter check, hold back one more small,
   most-recent slice *before* the full-data refit specifically for this
   recalibration/QA pass, at the cost of a small amount of data the
   production model never trains on.

Document which path was taken in Step 9.

## Step 10 definition-of-done — Model Integration

- Scoring contract on every record: `record_id`, `pred_1`, `score_norm`
  (locked 1–1000 map), `decision` at the **locked** Step 6 operating
  point, `model_version`, `scored_at`.
- Loads `champion_final` and train-fit preprocessors only — no refit on
  production data.
- Serving mode recorded (batch / API / embed) and honors the up-front
  cloud decision.
- TreeSHAP explanations for the actioned cohort (default), not LIME.
- Written to `configs/integration.yaml` plus `src/` scorer.

## Step 11 definition-of-done — Model Monitoring

- Watch three things: input drift vs **train** features, score drift vs
  the **test-window** `pred_1` / `score_norm` baseline, labeled
  performance at the locked operating point **once outcomes exist**.
- Label lag is explicit; do not invent labeled metrics before outcomes
  can exist.
- Alerts produce `ok` / `investigate` / `retrain_candidate` — they do
  not silently retrain or retune the threshold.
- Written to `configs/monitoring.yaml`, job under `src/`, reports under
  `reports/monitoring/`.

## Step 12 definition-of-done — Periodic Retraining

- Trigger recorded (monitor verdict, cadence, or ad hoc).
- New data, then a **slid OOT** split (new most-recent slice = new test).
  Explore the new train window only.
- Default: same champion family, **held** hyperparameters, same feature
  definitions. Re-tune or re-bake-off only if the user confirms.
- Old shipped model is scored on the **new** test window; promote only if
  the candidate beats it on the `/ml-define` success metric **and** still
  meets the business constraint on a fresh operating table.
- On promote: new operating point, TreeSHAP, calibration, Step 8.5 refit,
  new `model_version`, previous artifact kept for rollback. Loops back to
  steps 2/3 on the next trigger.

## Reference

See `PROJECT.md` in each project repo for that project's specific timeline,
cloud decision, and per-step status. See `templates/report_template.md`
(or `report_template.docx` when supplied) for the exact section headers
`/ml-document` should mirror. Step skills live in `.cursor/skills/` (and,
after a global install, `~/.cursor/skills/`).
